    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable to feel frustrated after investing significant time into two rounds of interviews for a leadership role, especially when you have strong qualifications and haven't received the feedback you requested. \n\nHowever, looking at the situation objectively, **making an issue out of the panel’s gender composition is unlikely to help you and carries substantial professional risk.** \n\nHere is an analysis of the situation and guidance on how to navigate it:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is an all-female panel evidence of bias?\n\n**In isolation, no.** \n\n* **Panel selection:** Interview panels are typically composed based on organizational roles (e.g., Head of HR, Managing Director, Operations Director, or practice leads). Unless you have specific evidence that the panel was deliberately engineered to exclude men or that anti-male bias influenced the scoring, an all-female panel is no more inherently biased than an all-male panel would be.\n* **Legal standard:** In employment law, proving discrimination requires evidence of disparate treatment or explicit bias. The demographic makeup of a panel combined with the outcome of a decision does not meet that standard.\n* **Perception vs. reality:** For decades, candidates have sat before all-male panels; while demographic imbalance can sometimes raise concerns about unconscious bias, the composition alone does not constitute proof of an unfair process.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why a candidate with 8 years of experience might be chosen over one with 15\n\nFor a **\"Head of Group\"** position, technical competence is only a baseline; commercial and interpersonal capabilities often drive the final decision:\n\n* **Seniority vs. Suitability:** Once a candidate holds a chartered license and meets the minimum technical threshold (8 years is typically sufficient for senior management in many consultancies), additional years of design work do not automatically guarantee the job.\n* **Client Relationships & Business Development:** The announcement highlighted that the successful candidate has *\"excellent client relationships.\"* For a department head, bringing in work, retaining key accounts, and generating revenue are often prioritized over raw design experience.\n* **Leadership and Culture:** Selection often hinges on communication style, strategic vision for the team, and interview performance on leadership scenarios rather than project count.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The 2-week delay in written feedback\n\nWhile waiting two weeks is frustrating, it is not uncommon:\n\n* **HR Caution:** Many corporate HR departments are hesitant to provide detailed *written* feedback to unsuccessful finalists precisely because candidates sometimes scrutinize written statements for grounds to dispute the decision.\n* **Process Delays:** Finalizing contracts, onboarding the successful candidate, or simply standard corporate backlog can delay post-interview debriefs.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Risks of \"making something of it\"\n\nRaising the issue of the panel's gender—either directly with the firm or publicly—carries significant downsides:\n\n* **High reputational risk:** Structural engineering is a relatively small, interconnected discipline. Challenging a hiring outcome based on gender demographics without concrete proof can damage your professional reputation across the industry.\n* **Defensive response:** It will immediately close the door to constructive feedback and eliminate any chance of future collaboration or opportunities with that consultancy.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite, focused follow-up:**  \n   Instead of demanding written feedback, offer a short phone call. Companies are often much more candid and willing to share constructive insights over a 10-minute phone conversation than in writing.\n   > *\"Dear [HR/Hiring Manager], I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback regarding the Head of Structural Engineering position. I am keen to understand what areas I can strengthen as I look toward future leadership opportunities. If a brief 10-minute call is easier than a written summary, I would be very grateful for your time.\"*\n\n2. **Evaluate the interview focus:**  \n   Reflect on your two interviews: Did the discussion lean toward technical project delivery, or toward commercial strategy, team mentorship, and client acquisition? Identifying where the emphasis lay can help clarify what the panel prioritized.\n\n3. **Channel your energy forward:**  \n   Making the final shortlist of three for a department head role confirms that your CV, chartership, and experience are competitive at an executive level. Focus on leveraging that momentum for your next opportunity.\n",
